Denoise marker-gene reads into amplicon sequence variants (ASVs, named
\code{ASV_1}, \code{ASV_2}, ...) and assemble them into a
\code{\link[phyloseq:phyloseq-class]{phyloseq::phyloseq-class()}} object with an \code{otu_table} of per-sample
abundances and the representative sequence of each variant in the
\code{refseq} slot. Three denoising engines are available:
\itemize{
\item \code{method = "vsearch"} (default): \href{https://github.com/torognes/vsearch}{vsearch}
UNOISE3 denoising (\code{--cluster_unoise}), producing zOTUs. Works on
fasta, fastq and \code{dna_seq} inputs.
\item \code{method = "swarm"}: \href{https://github.com/torognes/swarm}{SWARM} with
\code{d = 1} (fastidious by default). Works on fasta, fastq and \code{dna_seq}
inputs.
\item \code{method = "dada2"}: the classical \href{https://benjjneb.github.io/dada2/}{dada2}
denoising pipeline (\code{dada2::dada()} with \code{selfConsist = TRUE} by
default, overridable through \code{...}), followed by de-novo chimera
removal (\code{dada2::removeBimeraDenovo()}). Requires fastq files (quality
scores drive the dada2 error model); reads containing non-ACGT bases
are discarded with a message.
}

For fasta/fastq input, reads are first dereplicated per sample with
vsearch, then pooled and dereplicated again so that abundances per
sample can be rebuilt after denoising (see the details of
\code{\link[=cluster_reads]{cluster_reads()}}). Use \code{\link[=cluster_reads]{cluster_reads()}} instead of this function to
cluster reads into similarity-threshold OTUs (vsearch \code{--cluster_size}
or SWARM with \code{d > 1}).
}
